## Context

Fifth tranche (tracker 0550): the ratified arbitration lands last,
deliberately — before it, FIFO-per-class is correct behavior, just less
fresh. This tranche also carries the scheduling property suite: the real
scheduler run in virtual time on the synthetic library, replacing the
rejected Python simulation (see tracker).

## Relevant files

- SPEC.md §5.2.5 (priority tree; r = 3 ratified; drain bound and its
  scope; band 0 before band 1; the two units outside the yield interval),
  §5.2.3 (bands, K), DECISIONS.md 2026-09-01 (interleave ruling and its
  three stated semantics), ticket 0080 (adjacent per-item question — do
  not fold it in here).

## Actions

1. The interleave: counter draw, backfill on 0 mod (r+1); work-conserving
   fall-through; no carried credit; counter advances only on body-class
   dispatches.
2. Band 0 / band 1 in the dispatch ORDER BY, K per SPEC §5.2.3.
3. Property suite in virtual time on the synthetic library, red first:
   - backfill progresses under sustained fresh arrival (no starvation);
   - the monster item does not monopolize (band cap holds — heads of all
     items indexed before any tail);
   - fresh-lane snapshot drains within ceil(N/r) × (r+1) × quantum in
     body-class slots;
   - foreground activity idles every worker within one micro-batch.

## Test

- The suite above IS the test. Each property demonstrated red against a
  deliberately broken scheduler variant first.

## Verification

- [ ] Observed fresh:backfill service ratio over a mixed run is 3:1
      within tolerance when both lanes are non-empty
- [ ] Empty-lane fall-through: backfill gets 100 % when fresh is empty

## Invariants

- All earlier tranches' tests stay green; byte-identical MiniLM check.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Interleave + bands + property suite merged on a fork branch
